ct will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $196$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{96}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-1.4)^{2}=1.96\\,.$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443068 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443079 1069 "q-185190" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443079 1071 "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number, ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443079 1072 #uuid "5df64b77-ebca-5b73-8314-1b0b4f096670" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443079 1074 "To work out ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ we multiply $\\frac{3}{2}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443058 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443080 1069 "q-185112" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443080 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443080 1071 "$(-4)^{2}=${{field-1}}" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443080 1072 #uuid "63757b4a-7f76-56b7-8980-04eae99b6886" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443080 1074 "To work out $(-4)^{2},$ we multiply $-4$ by itself:\n\n$$\n(-4)^{2}=(-4)\\cdot (-4)=16\n$$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443052 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443081 1069 "q-185174" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443081 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443081 1071 "Expressed as a fraction, ${(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443081 1072 #uuid "668fdcb5-56eb-5d4c-8344-17b223fde624" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443081 1074 "To work out ${(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2},$ we multiply $-\\frac{3}{4}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =(-\\frac{3}{4})\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443061 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443082 1069 "q-185103" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443082 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443082 1071 "Expressed as a fraction, ${(\\frac{1}{11})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443082 1072 #uuid "8752fb1d-1037-5b8f-989d-db4a62d747cd" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443082 1074 "To work out ${(\\frac{1}{11})}^{2},$ we multiply $\\frac{1}{11}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{11})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{11}\\cdot \\frac{1}{11}=\\frac{1}{121}\n$$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443053 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443083 1069 "q-185072" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443083 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443083 1071 "${7}^{2}=${{field-1}}" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443083 1072 #uuid "92b0451f-5a0f-52d2-ba64-98d0de8b76eb" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443083 1074 "To work out ${7}^{2},$ we multiply $7$ by itself:\n\n$$\n{7}^{2}=7\\cdot 7=49\n$$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443055 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443084 1069 "q-185105" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443084 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443084 1071 "Expressed as a fraction, ${(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443084 1072 #uuid "a5930e78-0883-51ee-8797-7a8e907a4b0e" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443084 1074 "To work out ${(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2},$ we multiply $\\frac{3}{5}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{9}{25}\n$$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443085 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443054 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443085 1069 "q-99424" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443085 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443085 1071 "${13}^{2}=$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443085 1072 #uuid "b5496cbf-43fd-56ac-8687-6111e219fb1f" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443085 1074 "To work out ${13}^{2},$ we multiply $13$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $13\\times 13=169.$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443086 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443067 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443086 1069 "q-185204" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443086 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443086 1071 "Expressed as a decimal in its simplest form, $(5.3)^{2}=${{field-1}}." 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443086 1072 #uuid "c5bedd10-8ffa-5ad9-bdb2-5aa9ca8ef32b" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443086 1074 "We need to calculate the product of $5.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{5.3}^{2}=(5.3)\\cdot (5.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{1} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $53\\times 53=2\\,809.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($5.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($5.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $2\\,809$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n28\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{09}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${5.3}^{2}=28.09\\,.$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443070 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443087 1069 "q-185196" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443087 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443087 1071 "Expressed as a decimal in its simplest form, $(-0.3)^{2}=${{field-1}}." 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443087 1072 #uuid "e04ccdd3-f28d-58f9-a502-15fb5e93205e" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443087 1074 "We need to calculate the product of $-0.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-0.3)^{2}=(-0.3)\\cdot (-0.3)=(0.3)\\cdot (0.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n3\\times 3=9.\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($0.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($0.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $9$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n0\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{09}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-0.3)^{2}=0.09\\,.$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443069 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443088 1069 "q-104519" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443088 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443088 1071 "${25}^{2}=$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443088 1072 #uuid "e84b722e-1f7b-5e51-918b-729f6c73a9a8" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443088 1074 "To work out \n${25}^{2},$\n we multiply \n$25$\n by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{1} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $25\\times 25=625.$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443066 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443089 1069 "q-69409" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443089 1071 "${1.6}^{2}=$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443089 1072 #uuid "f176b794-be06-5dbc-b5c3-35aaeec4668c" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443089 1074 "We need to calculate the product of $1.6$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{1.6}^{2}=(1.6)\\cdot (1.6)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{3}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16\\times 16=256.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($1.6$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($1.6$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $256$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{56}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${1.6}^{2}=2.56\\,.$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443057 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443090 1069 "q-99691" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443090 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443090 1071 "$(-14)^{2}=$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443090 1072 #uuid "f5d1c8d9-881c-5cab-bdcb-86d5598dd1ea" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443090 1074 "Since the product of two negative numbers gives a positive number, we have\n\n$$\n(-14)^{2}=(-14)\\cdot (-14)=14\\cdot 14\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $14\\cdot 14:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(-14)^{2}={14}^{2}=196.\n$$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443065 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443091 1069 "q-185191" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443091 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443091 1071 "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number, ${(-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}=${{field-1}}." 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443091 1072 #uuid "fa89591c-42d2-57e3-bfd4-69e840192bd4" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true] [17592186443091 1074 "To work out ${(-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2},$ we multiply $-\\frac{9}{2}$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ (-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{2}) & = \\\\ \\frac{9\\cdot 9}{2\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ \\frac{81}{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534897 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-07d009e2-b0fa-508e-a552-f6df2d492b5c" 17592186442996 "answer-0b76b398